# Vercel Speed Insights Implementation

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for this Next.js project following the latest official documentation from Vercel.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` version 2.0.0
- Added to dependencies in `package.json`
- Updated `package-lock.json` with the new dependency tree

### 2. Code Changes
**Modified: `app/layout.tsx`**
- Added import statement: `import { SpeedInsights } from "@vercel/speed-insights/next";`
- Added `<SpeedInsights />` component in the `<body>` tag, right after the existing `<Analytics />` component

## Implementation Details

### Framework-Specific Configuration
This project uses Next.js with the App Router architecture. Following the official Vercel documentation:
- Used the Next.js-specific import path: `@vercel/speed-insights/next`
- Placed the component in the root layout file (`app/layout.tsx`) within the body element
- No additional configuration was required

### Integration Pattern
The SpeedInsights component was added alongside the existing Analytics component, maintaining consistency with the project's existing Vercel integrations:
```tsx
<body className="min-h-full flex flex-col font-sans">
  {children}
  <Analytics />
  <SpeedInsights />
</body>
```

## Verification Steps Completed

✅ **Build Verification**: Successfully ran `npm run build` with no errors
✅ **Linting**: Passed `npm run lint` with no issues  
✅ **Type Safety**: TypeScript compilation successful
✅ **Dependencies**: Updated lock file to ensure reproducible builds

## How It Works

Once deployed to Vercel, the Speed Insights component will:
1. Automatically inject tracking scripts to measure Web Vitals and performance metrics
2. Collect real user monitoring (RUM) data
3. Send performance data to the Vercel dashboard for analysis
4. Provide insights on Core Web Vitals including LCP, FID, CLS, FCP, and TTFB

No manual configuration or environment variables are required. The integration is fully automatic when deployed to Vercel.

## Files Modified
- `website/app/layout.tsx` - Added SpeedInsights import and component
- `website/package.json` - Added @vercel/speed-insights dependency
- `website/package-lock.json` - Updated dependency lock file

## Notes
- The implementation follows the exact pattern used for the existing `@vercel/analytics` package already in the project
- Speed Insights will begin collecting data immediately upon deployment to Vercel
- No breaking changes were introduced; all existing functionality remains intact
